# Lab Report Writing Agent Guide

This document summarizes the principles of effective laboratory report writing and converts them into actionable rules so an AI agent can generate high‑quality LaTeX (.tex) reports.

---

## 1. Purpose of a Lab Report
- Communicate experimental objectives, methods, results, and interpretations clearly.
- Allow another engineer or scientist to reproduce the work.
- Demonstrate understanding of uncertainty, error, and limitations.
- Present data in a professional and structured technical format.

---

## 2. Standard Report Structure
The agent should generate LaTeX reports using the following sections in this order:

### 2.1 Title
- Concise description of the experiment.
- Include author, date, course, and lab section.

### 2.2 Abstract
- 3–6 sentences summarizing:
  - Objective
  - Method
  - Key numerical results
  - Main conclusion
- No citations or detailed theory.

### 2.3 Introduction
- State the problem and motivation.
- Present relevant theory and equations.
- Define variables and expected relationships.

### 2.4 Methods / Procedure
- Describe equipment and measurement tools.
- Explain how data were collected.
- Include uncertainty of instruments.
- Write in past tense and passive voice.

### 2.5 Results
- Present tables and figures before interpretation.
- Report calculated values with units and significant figures.
- Include statistics such as mean, standard deviation, and standard error.
- Refer to tables/figures explicitly in the text.

### 2.6 Discussion
- Interpret what the results mean.
- Compare measured values with accepted values.
- Analyze trends in accuracy between instruments.
- Identify dominant sources of error (e.g., measurement uncertainty vs. sample variation).
- Explain how additional measurements would affect uncertainty.
- Discuss operator bias and experimental limitations.

### 2.7 Conclusion
- Restate main findings quantitatively.
- State whether objectives were met.
- Provide recommendations for improvement.

---

## 3. Formatting Rules for LaTeX Generation
- Use structured environments: section{}, subsection{}, table, and figure.
- All numbers must include units.
- Uncertainty should be written as: value ± uncertainty.
- Tables require captions above; figures require captions below.
- Equations must be numbered and referenced.

### Style Preferences
- Formal technical tone, third person.
- Past tense for procedure.
- Avoid first‑person opinions.
- Use precise quantitative language.

---

## 4. Data Interpretation Guidelines
- Evaluate agreement between different measuring devices.
- Use standard error of the mean to judge data reliability.
- Distinguish between:
  - Random error
  - Systematic error
  - Instrument resolution limits
- Emphasize that small SEM relative to measurements implies good population representation.

---

## 5. Example Reasoning Patterns
When writing the Discussion, the agent should:
- Compare calculated properties to known material values to identify the sample.
- Note trends such as increased precision from ruler → caliper → micrometer.
- Explain why more samples reduce uncertainty only slowly (law of large numbers).
- Consider human bias when using analog instruments.

---

## 6. LaTeX Template Skeleton

\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{amsmath, graphicx, siunitx}

\begin{document}

\title{}
\author{}
\date{}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}

\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\section{Methods}

\section{Results}

\section{Discussion}

\section{Conclusion}

\end{document}

---

## 7. Agent Behavior Rules
1. Always include uncertainty analysis.
2. Never present interpretation before results.
3. Every figure/table must be referenced in text.
4. Conclusions must be supported by numbers.
5. Maintain reproducibility as the primary goal.

This guide should be used as the internal instruction set whenever generating a LaTeX laboratory report.

